ACCEPT HEADER 
=============
👉 Sent by  : the client
👉 Meaning  : these are the formats I’m willing to receive.
👉 Handling : The server may ignore Accept  : ✔ technically allowed, ❌ but not ideal
            
    situation : < type not handled > : HTTP/1.1 406 Not Acceptable                                                   HTTP/1.1 406 Not Acceptable 
        
RELATED HEADERS
===============
Accept          : Indicates preferred media types (MIME types)	
                    Accept: application/json, text/html;q=0.8

Accept-Language	: Preferred languages	
                    Accept-Language: en-US, fr;q=0.7

Accept-Encoding	: Preferred content encodings / compression	
                    Accept-Encoding: gzip, deflate, br

Accept-Charset	: Preferred character sets	
                    Accept-Charset: utf-8, iso-8859-1;q=0.5

1. Basic Syntax
===============
Accept = #( media-range [ accept-params ] )

    Symbol            Meaning
    ======            =======
     #              : A comma-separated list 
    "media-range"   : text/html  (format is : type/subtype)
    "accept-params" : charset, 
    []              : optional
    ;               : end of value (query(s) can follow but are optional)
    ,               : new value follows 
    
media-range = ( "*/*"
              | type "/*"
              | type "/" subtype
              ) *( ";" parameter )

accept-params = weight *( accept-ext )

weight = ";q=" qvalue
qvalue = ( "0" [ "." 0*3DIGIT ] )
       | ( "1" [ "." 0*3("0") ] )

e.g. Accept = text/html;q=0.5, text/*;q=0.3

2. Media Ranges
===============

The Accept header lists media ranges:

            1) Exact type      :           Accept: text/html
            2) Type wildcard   :           Accept: text/*
            3) Global wildcard :           Accept: */*


3. Parameters
=============

Media types can include parameters:

            Accept: text/html; charset=utf-8

These parameters are part of the media-range matching (not preference weighting).

Also see file headers/request/media_types_and_parameters


4. Quality Values (q)
=====================

Each media range can include a quality factor (q) to express preference:

Accept: text/html;q=1.0, application/json;q=0.9, */*;q=0.8
Rules:

Range: 0 to 1

Default: q=1.0 if omitted

q=0 means not acceptable

Examples:

Accept: text/html, application/json;q=0.5


5. Matching Rules (Server-Side Selection)
=========================================
When the server chooses a response type, it follows:

Step 1: Find all matching media ranges

    Example:

    Accept: text/*;q=0.7, text/html;q=0.9
    Content-Type: text/html

    Matches:

        text/html (exact)
        text/* (wildcard)

Step 2: Choose the most specific match

    Priority:

       1) type/subtype (most specific)
       2) type/*
       3) */* (least specific)

Step 3: Apply highest q value

    If multiple matches:

        Pick the one with highest q

Step 4: Tie-breaking

    If still tied:

    1) Prefer the most specific
    2) Then order of appearance (in practice)

6. Precedence Example
=====================
Accept: text/*;q=0.5, text/html;q=0.8, */*;q=0.3

For text/html:

    Match	    q	    Specificity
    =====       =       ===========
    text/html	0.8	    highest
    text/*	    0.5	    medium
    /	        0.3	    lowest

    → Result: text/html;q=0.8


7. Extension Parameters (accept-ext)
====================================
Rarely used, but allowed:

    Accept: text/html;q=0.9;level=1

Rules:

    1) Must come after q
    2) Not used for matching, only for extensions

8. Special Cases
==================

1) No Accept header

    → Means:        */* i.e. Client accepts anything

2) Explicit rejection
    
    Accept: text/html;q=0

    → Client explicitly refuses text/html

3) Multiple headers

    Accept: text/html
    Accept: application/json

    → Equivalent to:

    Accept: text/html, application/json


9. HTTP/1.0 vs HTTP/1.1 Differences
===================================

HTTP/1.0
========
Basic support for:
                    1) media types
                    2) wildcards

No formal definition of q values (but widely implemented)

HTTP/1.1
========
Fully specifies:

                    1) q weighting
                    2) matching algorithm
                    3) precedence rules
                    4) extension parameters



10. Real-World Example
=======================

Accept: text/html, application/xhtml+xml;q=0.9, application/json;q=0.8, */*;q=0.5

Preference order:

text/html

application/xhtml+xml

application/json

anything else


11. Server Behavior (Important)
================================

If no acceptable type is found:

HTTP/1.1 406 Not Acceptable

However, many servers:

Ignore Accept

Return a default (often text/html or JSON)


12. Summary Rules
==================

Default is */* if missing

Most specific match wins

Highest q wins

q=0 means forbidden

Order only matters as last tie-breaker

Parameters before q are part of the type

Parameters after q are extensions

HANDLING ACCEPT HEADER
======================

For a webserv project, you usually don’t need full content negotiation.

    Practical approach
    ==================
    1) Ignore Accept header in most cases

    2) Serve based on file extension:

        .html → text/html
        .css  → text/css
        .js   → application/javascript

    3) Only return 406 Not Acceptable if you explicitly implement negotiation

    Charset Handling
    ================
    You can safely default to:

            text/html; charset=UTF-8
            text/css; charset=UTF-8
            application/javascript; charset=UTF-8

    You do NOT need to parse charset from Accept for this project.

    Mapping Table (you’ll likely implement this)
    ============================================
    Example:

            Extension   	Content-Type
            =========       ============
            .html	        text/html
            .css	        text/css
            .js             application/javascript
            .json	        application/json
            .png	        image/png
            .jpg	        image/jpeg
            .svg	        image/svg+xml
            .ico	        image/x-icon
            .woff2	        font/woff2

    
